            <fr:title text="(P4): the maximum of umod over compositions">(P4): the maximum of umod over compositions</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>theorem</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">growth</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="status">proven</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>(P4). <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \max _{c \vdash  n} \operatorname {umod}(\mathfrak {n}_{c}) = \lfloor  n(n+6)/12 \rfloor ,
]]></fr:tex>
attained exactly by the period-6 maximizer shapes.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Mechanism.</html:strong> Poset-interval induction (H10--H12): the argmax is shown
unimodal, so the maximum can be computed on the unimodal locus where
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0033/" title="(Deg-Opt): the grand formula for umod" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0033/" display-uri="ffi-0033" type="local">(Deg-Opt)</fr:link> is available in closed form, and the period-6 shapes
are exhibited as attaining it.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Consequence.</html:strong> With <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0037/" title="The growth theorem" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0037/" display-uri="ffi-0037" type="local">the growth theorem</fr:link> this makes the floor of
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0038/" title="The degree law" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0038/" display-uri="ffi-0038" type="local">the degree law</fr:link> unconditional at every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex>. It is also what
makes <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\lfloor  n(n+6)/12 \rfloor ]]></fr:tex> the right closed form to look for in the first
place.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0030/" title="z_u(c) and the modality formula" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0030/" display-uri="ffi-0030" type="local">z_u(c) and the modality formula</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0036/" title="(P6): the collapse identity and the period-6 structure" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0036/" display-uri="ffi-0036" type="local">(P6): the collapse identity and the period-6 structure</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>
              <html:em>Provenance: scratch_nineteenth.md Result (P4); certificates
mlaw_close.py, o2_symbolic and o2_singular.</html:em>
            </html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0026/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0026</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0026/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The class count kappa(U_n)">The class count kappa(U_n)</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>definition</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">growth</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>For the unitriangular group <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[U_{n} = U_{n}(\mathbb {F}_q)]]></fr:tex>, let
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\kappa (U_{n}) = \kappa (U_{n}(\mathbb {F}_q))]]></fr:tex> denote its number of conjugacy classes.
<html:strong>Higman's conjecture</html:strong>: <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\kappa (U_{n})]]></fr:tex> is polynomial in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex> for each
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
            <html:p>Where polynomiality is known — <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n \le  16]]></fr:tex>, Pak--Soffer — write
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\deg  \kappa (U_{n})]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {lc} \kappa (U_{n})]]></fr:tex> for the degree and the leading coefficient.
The growth program is the study of these two numbers:
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0038/" title="The degree law" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0038/" display-uri="ffi-0038" type="local">the degree law</fr:link> for the degree,
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0039/" title="The leading-term law" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0039/" display-uri="ffi-0039" type="local">the leading-term law</fr:link> for the leading coefficient.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0001/" title="Setup: conjugation over a finite field" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0001/" display-uri="ffi-0001" type="local">Setup: conjugation over a finite field</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>
              <html:em>Provenance: Higman; Pak--Soffer App.~A for the known values.</html:em>
            </html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0039/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0039</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0039/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="The leading-term law">The leading-term law</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>conjecture</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">growth</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="area">frontier</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="status">open</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>For <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n \ge  8]]></fr:tex>: <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \deg  \kappa (U_{n}) = \lfloor  n(n+6)/12 \rfloor ,
  \qquad 
  \operatorname {lc} \kappa (U_{n}) = n / \gcd (n, 6).
]]></fr:tex></html:p>
            <html:p>The unique exception in all data is the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n = 7]]></fr:tex> sport,
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {lc} \kappa (U_{7}) = 8 = 7 + 1]]></fr:tex>: seven maximizers on formula — <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(2,3,2)]]></fr:tex> is the
seventh — plus one extra class from the non-unimodal tie <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(1,2,1,2,1)]]></fr:tex>.
Verified against every published value, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n \le  16]]></fr:tex>, with twelve
straight <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(\deg , \operatorname {lc})]]></fr:tex> matches for <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n = 6 \dots  17]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Status.</html:strong> The unimodal half is a theorem, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0040/" title="The M-law: the unimodal side of the leading-term law" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0040/" display-uri="ffi-0040" type="local">the M-law</fr:link>. What is
missing is the passage from parabolic argmax counts to <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {lc} \kappa (U_{n})]]></fr:tex>,
which is <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0041/" title="(TOP-X): rigidity of maximal modality" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0041/" display-uri="ffi-0041" type="local">(TOP-X)</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0026/" title="The class count kappa(U_n)" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0026/" display-uri="ffi-0026" type="local">The class count kappa(U_n)</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0038/" title="The degree law" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0038/" display-uri="ffi-0038" type="local">The degree law</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0040/" title="The M-law: the unimodal side of the leading-term law" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0040/" display-uri="ffi-0040" type="local">The M-law: the unimodal side of the leading-term law</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0041/" title="(TOP-X): rigidity of maximal modality" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0041/" display-uri="ffi-0041" type="local">(TOP-X): rigidity of maximal modality</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>
              <html:em>Provenance: scratch_nineteenth.md; Pak--Soffer for the published
values.</html:em>
            </html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0029/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>ffi-0029</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0029/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="Theorem D: the modality cap">Theorem D: the modality cap</fr:title>
            <fr:taxon>theorem</fr:taxon>
            <fr:meta name="area">growth</fr:meta>
            <fr:meta name="status">proven</fr:meta>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>Theorem D. For every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[U]]></fr:tex>-stable irreducible <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[W]]></fr:tex>: <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \operatorname {umod}(W) \le  f_B(B \cdot  \overline {W}) + (n-1)
            \le  \operatorname {mod}(B : \mathfrak {n}_{n}) + (n-1),
]]></fr:tex>
and consequently <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\deg  \kappa (U_{n}) \le  (n-1) + \operatorname {mod}(B : \mathfrak {n}_{n})]]></fr:tex>
unconditionally.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:strong>Role in the skeleton.</html:strong> This is the bridge. It is what ties the growth program to the same
Borel-orbit geometry that carries <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0009/" title="The height conjecture" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0009/" display-uri="ffi-0009" type="local">the height conjecture</fr:link>: the
invariant <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {mod}(B : \mathfrak {n}_{n})]]></fr:tex> bounding Higman's degree is the same one the
height ladder spends. Paired with the lower bound of
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0037/" title="The growth theorem" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0037/" display-uri="ffi-0037" type="local">the growth theorem</fr:link> it pins <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\deg  \kappa (U_{n})]]></fr:tex> from both sides,
which is the content of <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0038/" title="The degree law" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0038/" display-uri="ffi-0038" type="local">the degree law</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p><html:em>Uses:</html:em> <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0026/" title="The class count kappa(U_n)" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0026/" display-uri="ffi-0026" type="local">The class count kappa(U_n)</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0027/" title="Borel modality" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0027/" display-uri="ffi-0027" type="local">Borel modality</fr:link>, <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0028/" title="U-modality" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0028/" display-uri="ffi-0028" type="local">U-modality</fr:link>.</html:p>
            <html:p>
              <html:em>Provenance: lc_law.md Theorem D.</html:em>
            </html:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors />
            <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/sec-growth/</fr:uri>
            <fr:display-uri>sec-growth</fr:display-uri>
            <fr:route>/ffi/sec-growth/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="finite-field-invariants: a reference forest › The growth program"><fr:link href="/ffi/index/" title="finite-field-invariants: a reference forest" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/index/" display-uri="index" type="local">finite-field-invariants: a reference forest</fr:link> › The growth program</fr:title>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>Higman's class count, reached through the same Borel-orbit
  geometry. The chain from the definition of modality to the leading
  term of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\kappa (U_{n})]]></fr:tex> is complete except for one inequality, and the
  degree half of it is a theorem for every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
            <html:ul><html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0026/" title="The class count kappa(U_n)" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0026/" display-uri="ffi-0026" type="local">The class count</fr:link> — Higman's program.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0027/" title="Borel modality" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0027/" display-uri="ffi-0027" type="local">Borel modality</fr:link> — modality of the Borel on its
    nilradical.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0028/" title="U-modality" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0028/" display-uri="ffi-0028" type="local"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[U]]></fr:tex>-modality</fr:link> — modality of the unipotent
    group.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0029/" title="Theorem D: the modality cap" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0029/" display-uri="ffi-0029" type="local">Theorem D</fr:link> — the bridge to the class count.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0030/" title="z_u(c) and the modality formula" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0030/" display-uri="ffi-0030" type="local"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[z_u(c)]]></fr:tex></fr:link> — the generic centralizer dimension.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0031/" title="The thread algebra" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0031/" display-uri="ffi-0031" type="local">The thread algebra</fr:link> — the algebra that computes it.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0032/" title="The graded count for z_u (H4)" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0032/" display-uri="ffi-0032" type="local">The graded count</fr:link> — the graded count and the defect.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0033/" title="(Deg-Opt): the grand formula for umod" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0033/" display-uri="ffi-0033" type="local">(Deg-Opt)</fr:link> — the closed form.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0034/" title="(P4): the maximum of umod over compositions" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0034/" display-uri="ffi-0034" type="local">(P4)</fr:link> — the maximum, and where it is attained.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0035/" title="(P5): the grand formula off the unimodal locus" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0035/" display-uri="ffi-0035" type="local">(P5)</fr:link> — the off-unimodal extension.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0036/" title="(P6): the collapse identity and the period-6 structure" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0036/" display-uri="ffi-0036" type="local">(P6)</fr:link> — the collapse identity and the origin of
    period 6.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0037/" title="The growth theorem" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0037/" display-uri="ffi-0037" type="local">The growth theorem</fr:link> — the unconditional lower bound.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0038/" title="The degree law" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0038/" display-uri="ffi-0038" type="local">The degree law</fr:link> — the degree.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0039/" title="The leading-term law" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0039/" display-uri="ffi-0039" type="local">The leading-term law</fr:link> — the leading coefficient.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0040/" title="The M-law: the unimodal side of the leading-term law" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0040/" display-uri="ffi-0040" type="local">The M-law</fr:link> — its proven half.</html:li>
    <html:li><fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0041/" title="(TOP-X): rigidity of maximal modality" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0041/" display-uri="ffi-0041" type="local">(TOP-X)</fr:link> — the rigidity statement it needs.</html:li></html:ul>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
      </fr:mainmatter>
    </fr:tree>
